    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s6-n62:modern-us-neutral:zh",
        "text": "不同物体会反射无线电波，这种现象广泛应用于定位技术。\n高灵敏度的接收机捕捉并放大物体反射回来的信号，从中获得物体位置的信息。\n\n**利用无线电波发现并确定目标位置的方法称为无线电定位**。\n无线电定位装置（无线电定位器①）包括发射部分和接收部分。\n本课介绍使用高频电振荡（$10^8$—$10^{11}$ 赫兹）的雷达，这个范围是教材讨论的例子，不是所有雷达的固定范围。\n\n发射器与具有方向性的天线相连，使辐射主要集中在一个方向。\n教材介绍的波长在 10 厘米以下的雷达，可以用抛物面反射天线形成窄波束。\n米波段的雷达也可以采用由多个辐射单元组成的天线系统。\n\n这些单元发出的波相互干涉，在选定方向上加强，在其他方向上完全或部分抵消。\n天线的排列和各单元的振荡关系共同决定这种方向性，并不意味着其他所有方向的辐射都必然为零。\n\n① 无线电定位装置通常也称为雷达。\n\n反射回来的波可以由发射天线接收，也可以由另一副定向接收天线接收。\n这种具有明显方向性的辐射形成雷达波束，教材把它称为“射线”。\n在本课介绍的定向雷达中，可以根据接收到回波时的波束方向确定目标方向。\n\n脉冲雷达通过发射短脉冲并接收回波来测量目标距离。\n教材举出的脉冲持续时间为几百万分之一秒，相邻脉冲之间的间隔大约是脉冲持续时间的 1000 倍。\n接收机在发射脉冲之间的停顿中接收反射波；这些时间值是具体系统的例子，不是所有雷达的共同参数。\n\n测出无线电波到达目标再返回所用的总时间 $t$，就可以求出目标距离 $R$。\n在本课的近似计算中，无线电波在大气中的速度取为 $c=3\\times10^8$ 米/秒。\n往返路程是目标距离的两倍，因此：\n$R=\\frac{ct}{2}$\n\n发射的无线电波会衰减，返回接收机的回波通常很弱，需要放大。\n教材描述的接收机把信号放大到 1 万亿（$10^{12}$）倍，这是它所介绍的系统参数，不能理解为所有雷达的统一增益。\n发射强脉冲时，接收机需要与发射信号隔离或暂时停止接收，以免强信号干扰接收过程。\n\n教材描述的瞬时发射功率为几十、几百甚至几千千瓦。\n在它介绍的阴极射线管显示系统中，即使采取隔离措施，发射脉冲仍可能使匀速扫描的亮点偏移。\n这会在距离标尺的零刻度附近形成一个扰动（图 123）。\n\n亮点继续沿标尺匀速移动，接收到较弱的目标回波时再次偏移。\n两个扰动之间的距离与往返时间 $t$ 成正比，也与目标距离 $R$ 成正比，因此标尺可以直接按千米标定。\n\n图 123\n\n图 124\n\n图 123\n\n图 124\n\n适当的雷达系统可以发现几百公里外的轮船和飞机，实际探测距离取决于系统、目标和传播条件。\n雷达不依赖日光，在许多天气条件下也能工作，但天气对它的影响并不总是很小。\n机场雷达可以监视飞机的起飞和降落，地面人员通过无线电向飞行员传达安全飞行所需的信息。\n\n图 124 表示教材介绍的一种机场雷达天线装置。\n轮船和飞机也可以装有导航雷达。\n这类雷达可以在屏幕上显示散射无线电波的物体的位置，让操纵员看到周围地区的雷达图像。\n\n雷达的用途包括观察高层大气中的流星、探测云中的回波以及研究宇宙。\n航天器可以根据任务需要配备雷达，并非每一艘航天器都必须装有若干部雷达。\n科学史上的例子包括 1946 年在美国和匈牙利开展的月球无线电回波实验，以及 1961 年苏联科学家开展的金星无线电定位研究。\n\n对行星回波的测量可以用于确定天文距离，并为估算行星的自转周期提供信息。\n太阳系其他行星也可以成为雷达研究的对象。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s6-n62:modern-us-neutral:en",
        "text": "Different objects reflect radio waves, and this effect is widely used to locate objects.\nA sensitive receiver detects and amplifies the reflected signals to obtain information about an object's position.\n\n**Using radio waves to detect a target and determine its position is called radio location**.\nA radio-location system, or radar set①, includes a transmitter and a receiver.\nThis lesson describes radar using high-frequency electrical oscillations ($10^8$—$10^{11}$ hertz); this is the textbook's example range, not a fixed range for every radar system.\n\nThe transmitter is connected to a directional antenna that concentrates most of the radiation in a selected direction.\nFor the radar systems described here with wavelengths below 10 centimeters, a parabolic reflector antenna can form a narrow beam.\nMeter-wave radar can also use an antenna system made of several radiating elements.\n\nThe waves from these elements interfere, reinforcing one another in the selected direction and canceling fully or partly in other directions.\nThe arrangement of the antenna and the oscillations of its elements determine the directionality; radiation need not vanish in every other direction.\n\n① A radio-location system is usually called radar.\n\nThe reflected waves may be received by the transmitting antenna or by a separate directional receiving antenna.\nThe strongly directional radiation forms a radar beam, called a \"ray\" in the textbook.\nFor the directional radar described here, the beam's direction when an echo is received can indicate the target's direction.\n\nPulse radar measures distance by transmitting short pulses and receiving their echoes.\nIn the textbook's example, a pulse lasts a few millionths of a second, and the interval between pulses is about 1000 times the pulse duration.\nThe receiver detects reflected waves during the pauses between transmissions; these times are example system parameters, not values shared by every radar.\n\nMeasuring the total time $t$ for a radio wave to reach the target and return gives the target distance $R$.\nIn this lesson's approximate calculation, the wave speed in air is taken as $c=3\\times10^8$ meters per second.\nThe round-trip path is twice the target distance, so:\n$R=\\frac{ct}{2}$\n\nTransmitted radio waves lose strength, and the echo returning to the receiver is usually weak and must be amplified.\nThe textbook describes a receiver with amplification of 1 trillion ($10^{12}$); this is a parameter of the described system, not a universal radar gain.\nDuring a powerful transmitted pulse, the receiver must be isolated from the transmission or temporarily stop receiving to avoid interference from the strong signal.\n\nThe textbook describes instantaneous transmitter powers of tens, hundreds, or even thousands of kilowatts.\nIn its cathode-ray-tube display, a transmitted pulse may still deflect the uniformly scanning bright spot despite isolation measures.\nThis produces a disturbance near the zero mark of the distance scale (Figure 123).\n\nThe spot continues moving uniformly along the scale and is deflected again when the weaker target echo arrives.\nThe separation of the disturbances is proportional to the round-trip time $t$, and therefore to the target distance $R$, so the scale can be calibrated directly in kilometers.\n\nFigure 123\n\nFigure 124\n\nFigure 123\n\nFigure 124\n\nSuitable radar systems can detect ships and aircraft hundreds of kilometers away; the actual range depends on the system, target, and propagation conditions.\nRadar does not rely on daylight and can operate in many weather conditions, but weather effects are not always small.\nAirport radar can monitor takeoffs and landings while ground personnel send pilots information needed for safe flight by radio.\n\nFigure 124 shows one airport radar antenna arrangement described in the textbook.\nShips and aircraft can also carry navigation radar.\nSuch radar can display the positions of objects that scatter radio waves, giving the operator a radar image of the surrounding area.\n\nRadar applications include observing meteors in the upper atmosphere, detecting echoes from clouds, and studying space.\nSpacecraft may carry radar when their missions require it; not every spacecraft must carry several radar sets.\nHistorical examples include lunar radio-echo experiments carried out in the United States and Hungary in 1946, and radio-location studies of Venus carried out by scientists of the Soviet Union in 1961.\n\nMeasurements of planetary echoes can determine astronomical distances and provide information for estimating a planet's rotation period.\nOther planets in the solar system can also be studied by radar."
      }
